}\b\f0\fs24  What are Compound Sentences?\b0\par
  \lang9 A compound {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentence_(linguistics)" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\o "Sentence (linguistics)" }}{\fldrslt{sentence}}}\f0\fs24  is composed of at least two {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_clause" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\o "Independent clause" }}{\fldrslt{independent clauses}}}\f0\fs24 . It does not require a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dependent_clause" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\o "Dependent clause" }}{\fldrslt{dependent clause}}}\f0\fs24 . The clauses are joined by a coordinating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grammatical_conjunction" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\o "Grammatical conjunction" }}{\fldrslt{conjunction}}}\f0\fs24  (with or without a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comma_(punctuation)" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\o "Comma (punctuation)" }}{\fldrslt{comma}}}\f0\fs24 ), a correlative conjunction (with or without a comma), or a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semicolon" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\o "Semicolon" }}{\fldrslt{semicolon}}}\f0\fs24  that functions as a conjunction.\lang1033   \par
